# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PALETTE, AND WHY IT IS AN ARC AND NOT A LIST OF ANCHORS.
#
# The seven PUBLISHED swatches have only two hue families: Ubuntu orange at
# h 46, and four aubergines packed between h 331 and h 349, plus two greys that
# carry no hue at all. A design that picks one published swatch per desktop can
# tell TWO desktops apart, not ten. So hue here is a CONTINUOUS ARC through the
# brand rather than a table of it: every published colour lies on the arc, the
# ends are the two families, and any point between is a blend of two brand
# colours rather than an invented one.
#
# HOW WIDE THE ARC MAY BE IS THE OWNER'S CALL AND HE HAS WIDENED IT. "the brand
# family has aubergine, orange, white/black, and variations of aubergine that
# feel more bluish or more orangey. within these constraints, we can also
# broaden the hue variance intentionally and it should still feel ubuntu like."
#
# So the ninety degrees this file was built around was a fact about SEVEN
# SWATCHES and never a fact about what reads as Ubuntu. Worth saying plainly,
# because a constraint inherited from a measurement of the wrong population is
# indistinguishable from a constraint imposed by the brand, and this one shaped
# the entire design: it is why composition and weight had to carry the
# separation that hue could not.
#
# AND WIDENING IT IS NOT A TRADE. private/measure-background-arc.py reports both
# halves at once -- how far hue travels WITHIN one sheet, and how far apart the
# DESKTOPS sit -- because spreading the tints buys the first by spending the
# second. Measured at drift 1.0:
#
#     arc span      within one image      between four desktops
#     86 deg  (now)   21 deg                47 deg
#     102 deg         25 deg                73 deg
#     118 deg         33 deg                82 deg
#     136 deg         51 deg               101 deg
#
# The wider arc is better on BOTH columns at once, which a trade cannot be. The
# narrow arc was not balancing anything; it was simply costing separation.
# 118 degrees is the recommendation -- 136 reaches far enough down to risk a
# true blue, which is not an aubergine leaning bluish -- and it is left unapplied
# here only because he is mid-round on something else and a silent change of
# colour would make his answers unreadable.
# DERIVED FROM THE PALETTE ABOVE, not typed. The warm end IS Ubuntu Orange. The
# cool end is the bluest published aubergine, extended a little further into the
# blue on the owner's explicit permission to "broaden the hue variance
# intentionally" -- so the extension is a named quantity with a reason, and
# changing the brand's hex would move the arc with it.
# HOW FAR PAST THE BLUEST PUBLISHED AUBERGINE IS SAFE, AND THE NUMBER CAME FROM
# LOOKING, NOT FROM THE SWEEP. private/measure-background-arc.py says a wider
# arc is better on BOTH separation axes at once -- 118 degrees beats 86 on
# within-image spread AND on between-desktop distance -- and on that basis 118
# was recommended. Rendering it and looking refuted that: at 306 degrees and
# below the colours come out #311ac6 and #223d8a, which are blue and navy, not
# "variations of aubergine that feel more bluish". Several desktops at 118 have
# frankly blue regions.
#
# So the sweep was optimising hue separation while the thing hue separation
# stands for -- still reading as Ubuntu -- got worse, and no amount of the
# metric would have said so. 102 degrees is the widest that still looks like the
# brand to me; the owner is the judge of "physiologically feels related", so the
# span is offered to him rather than settled here.
# HOW FAR PAST THE PUBLISHED SWATCHES WE GO, AND THE OWNER SET THIS, NOT THE
# BRAND: "we're not ubuntu. we just want to look compatible with ubuntu. that
# means, if we slightly break their rules, nobody is going to breath down our
# necks and users won't complain as long as everything 'looks nice'." And a
# datum with it -- earlier, less careful builds that "veered more into the blue
# or sometimes into the yellow (instead of orange)... worked and felt natural".
#
# So blue at one end and yellow at the other are LIKED, not leaks to be clamped
# away, and three rounds of me tightening this (118 -> 102 -> 88) were caution
# overshooting. The gamut limit is still real and physical -- a dark saturated
# violet is not in sRGB and comes back blue -- but "the realised hue left the
# arc" is only a defect if the arc is a hard boundary, and he has said it is
# not. It is a guide now.
COOL_EXTENSION = 34.9                # degrees past the bluest aubergine: BLUE
# THE WARM END STOPS AT UBUNTU ORANGE, AND THE REASON IS A CONSTRAINT WE CANNOT
# LIFT. The owner: "you mentioned a few times you pushed into yellow, but
# honestly the background was just purples and blues." He is right and I was
