# LIGHTNESS RELIEF: darker and lighter SECTIONS within one picture, placed at
# random and blended softly, on top of everything else. The owner, on a sheet
# that was light all over: "the brightness in general is not bad per se, but it
# should be used as a tool for variation. add darker section and sections like
# this randomly (and softly blending) in the same image."
#
# Nothing above can do that, which is why it is a layer and not a setting. The
# ladder moves a WHOLE desktop's lightness; the shades are painted UNDER the hue
# ramp, which repaints much of each edge -- measured on the round-2 winner, the
# middle 90% of a picture's pixels span only 8.7 L* units. So a lightness
# section has to be painted after the ramp, and it has to change lightness
# without undoing the ramp's hue:
#
#   DARK sections MULTIPLY by a mid-lightness colour in the dye's hue, at
#   RELIEF_DARK_L. Multiplying can only darken, and it keeps most of the hue of
#   whatever is underneath, so a dark section over the blue side stays bluish.
#   A NEUTRAL grey was tried first and looked at: it keeps the hue exactly but
#   takes the chroma down with the lightness, and the dark sections read as dirt
#   -- mud-brown over the warm side. Tinted by the dye they read as the same
#   material in shadow. It costs about 3 degrees of within-picture hue spread.
#
#   LIGHT sections are painted source-over in the dye's own hue at RELIEF_LIGHT_L.
#   A neutral SCREEN would keep the hue better but washes towards white -- the
#   "pastel" the owner already called out -- and, worse, it has no ceiling: a
#   lift can carry a pixel past the white-label limit. Source-over cannot. sRGB
#   decoding is convex, so a gamma-space mix of two colours is never brighter
#   than the brighter of them; with RELIEF_LIGHT_L at or under TOP_L the rendered
#   field stays under the ceiling the rest of this file already keeps. The cost
#   is that a light section leans towards the dye's hue where it is strong.
#
# ONE FIELD DECIDES BOTH, high for light and low for dark, so a light and a dark
# section never sit on top of each other and the picture passes through its own
# untouched colour between them -- that in-between is what makes it blend.
#
# RELIEF is the strength (0 = off, and then nothing is emitted at all: the
# shipped picture is byte-for-byte what it was). RELIEF_SCALE is the size of a
# section: at 1.0 one is a third to a half of the screen, smaller below 1.
# RELIEF_LIGHT_L
# None means TOP_L; setting it above TOP_L is for a deployment where no icon
# label ever sits on the background, and SPEC_CEILING has to be raised with it
# or "check" refuses -- deliberately, because that is the label ceiling.
RELIEF = 1.0
RELIEF_SCALE = 0.6
RELIEF_DARK_L = 40.0                 # the multiplying colour; lower is darker
RELIEF_LIGHT_L = None
RELIEF_CHROMA = 0.80                 # both colours' chroma fraction


def arc(t):
    """A hue at fractional position t along the brand arc.

    t IS CLAMPED, NOT WRAPPED, and that single word was a serious bug. The arc
    is an OPEN interval from the bluest aubergine to Ubuntu orange, not a circle
    -- the whole point is that hues outside it are off-brand. Wrapping t sent a
    position just past one end to the FAR end, so a tint drifting 15 degrees
    cooler than the coolest desktop came out ORANGE.

    Measured before the repair, at the 102 degree arc: seven of the eight rungs
    wrapped. Rung 0, the violet-most desktop, carried tints at 30.5 and 40.3 --
    Ubuntu orange -- and rung 6 carried one at 305.5, violet. The rendered mean
    hue spanned about 47 degrees of an 84 degree nominal arc and was NOT
    MONOTONIC in rung: rung 6 rendered cooler than rung 5. So the one thing this
    design exists to provide, a recognisable per-desktop hue, was scrambled at
    both ends.

    The guard that would have caught it is cheap and is now in cmd_check:
    rendered mean hue must be MONOTONIC in rung. It fires the moment drift
    crosses an end, which is exactly when the wrap bites."""
    lo, hi = arc_ends()
    return (lo + (hi - lo) * min(1.0, max(0.0, t))) % 360.0


